       A: Re-map priority to [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, noise_level] in that order; hard constraints on fatigue and noise are treated as non-negotiable overrides regardless of priority rank.
    2. resource_efficiency — direction: maximize, threshold: null
       Q: Is resource efficiency to be maximized without any ceiling, or is there a 'good enough' target above which further gains are not worth the risk (e.g., 1.4× the shift-start baseline)? Without an upper bound the optimizer may increase machine cycle rates by ~30–40%, potentially stressing unmeasured resources such as tooling wear, compressed-air consumption, or coolant temperature.
       A: Cap resource_efficiency improvement at 1.5× the rolling shift-start baseline; gains beyond this threshold carry diminishing returns and are not pursued.
    3. resource_efficiency — no minimum throughput floor
       Q: Must a minimum production quantity be delivered this shift regardless of efficiency? Without a floor, the optimizer can satisfy the efficiency objective by running very few high-yield, low-waste cycles and idling for the remainder of the shift, which maximizes the ratio but may miss customer demand commitments.
       A: Impose a minimum throughput floor equal to 90% of the historical shift-average output volume; the optimizer must meet this floor before pursuing efficiency gains.
    4. noise_level — threshold: 80 dB, shift duration unspecified
       Q: What is the planned duration of this shift? The 80 dB LAeq limit is calibrated to an 8-hour exposure (EU Directive 2003/10/EC); for a 10-hour shift the equivalent permissible level drops to approximately 77 dB, and for a 6-hour shift it rises to approximately 82 dB — altering which machine modes become feasible.
       A: Assume an 8-hour shift and hold the 80 dB LAeq threshold unchanged; if actual shift duration is confirmed to differ by more than 30 minutes, rescale the threshold using the equal-energy (3 dB exchange rate) rule before the optimizer runs.
    5. operator_fatigue — measurement lag vs. hard constraint enforcement
       Q: Fatigue is updated at most every 15 minutes, but the hard constraint triggers at 0.4. Should the optimizer apply a predictive safety buffer (e.g., throttle cycle rate when predicted fatigue reaches 0.35 to avoid overshoot before the next reading), or act only after a measured reading exceeds 0.4? The difference matters because a rapid fatigue spike between measurements could cause a regulatory violation before any intervention fires.
       A: Apply a 0.05 safety buffer: the optimizer throttles the cycle rate or schedules a micro-break whenever the most recent measured fatigue index reaches 0.35, treating 0.35 as the effective operational ceiling within each 15-minute measurement window.

