    4. operator_fatigue / measurement_granularity
       Q: The fatigue hard constraint is listed as ≤ 0.4 on a normalized index, but the sampling interval is unspecified ('continuously monitored or periodically sampled'). If fatigue spikes to 0.55 for 3 minutes during a demand burst and then drops back, is that a constraint violation? A time-averaged limit permits short spikes; an instantaneous limit does not—this changes feasibility significantly at high cycle rates.
       A: The constraint is enforced as a 15-minute rolling average. Any rolling window exceeding 0.4 triggers an immediate corrective action (speed reduction or enforced micro-break). Instantaneous readings above 0.6 are treated as an emergency stop regardless of average.
    5. noise_level / measurement_point_and_metric
       Q: The 80 dB ceiling is defined as L_eq at 'the operator workstation', but multi-operator cells may have 3–5 workstations with noise levels differing by up to 12 dB. Should the constraint apply to (a) the single highest-exposure workstation, (b) the average across all workstations, or (c) each workstation independently? Also, does the 80 dB limit apply only to L_eq, or must peak (C-weighted) noise also stay below a separate ceiling such as 135 dB_C?
       A: The 80 dB L_eq constraint is enforced at each individual operator workstation independently (worst-case station governs). C-weighted peak noise is capped at 135 dB_C per ISO 9612 defaults. If workstation-level sensors are unavailable, the nearest floor-level sensor within 1.5 m is used as a proxy.

