  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue_index (upper_bound), ambient_noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: FatigueIndex <= 0.4, Noise <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority list repeats 'Throughput' three times, but throughput is not defined as a KPI anywhere in the contract — should the priority order instead rank the three actual KPIs as [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue_index, ambient_noise_level], or did you intend a different ordering?
       A: Priority order is reset to [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue_index, ambient_noise_level], matching the stated primary and secondary objectives; hard constraints on fatigue and noise remain inviolable regardless of rank.
    2. resource_efficiency (maximize, no upper bound or saturation target)
       Q: With resource_efficiency uncapped, the optimiser will drive throughput and energy use up to the fatigue and noise ceilings — is there a minimum acceptable gain (e.g., ≥10% above today's baseline) beyond which further improvement is not required, or should the optimiser keep maximising without limit even if that means running both hard constraints at their limits continuously?
       A: No saturation cap is applied; the optimiser maximises resource_efficiency subject only to the hard constraints (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB), accepting that both ceilings may be sustained throughout the shift.
    3. context.time_horizon (shift_duration unspecified)
       Q: Operator fatigue accumulates over time and ambient noise is averaged as LAeq across the full shift — is the shift 8 hours, 10 hours, or 12 hours? A 0.4 fatigue ceiling over 12 hours is substantially more restrictive than over 8 hours and may reduce achievable efficiency by ~20%.
       A: An 8-hour shift is assumed; fatigue sampling and noise LAeq are evaluated over that full 8-hour window unless the scheduler provides an explicit shift record.
    4. operator_fatigue_index (measurement timing and enforcement mode)
       Q: Should the 0.4 fatigue limit be enforced as a continuous ceiling at every sample point throughout the shift (no transient spikes allowed), as a time-averaged value, or only as an end-of-shift reading? A continuous peak constraint is considerably more restrictive and may reduce peak throughput by ~15% compared with an end-of-shift reading.
       A: Fatigue is enforced as a continuous ceiling: the index must not exceed 0.4 at any sampled measurement point during the shift, consistent with the hard constraint being inviolable at all times.
    5. resource_efficiency (denominator — labor and overtime scope)
       Q: Does the labor component of the resource denominator include only scheduled hours at standard cost, or also overtime hours and any overtime pay premium? If the optimiser can implicitly extend working time to raise output value, the metric may appear to improve while actual operator exposure and fatigue risk both increase beyond the intended scope of this contract.
       A: Labor cost in the denominator covers all hours actually worked during the shift at standard rate only; overtime premium cost is excluded from the denominator, which makes additional hours appear cheaper — flag this assumption for review if overtime is a realistic scheduling option.

