======================================================================
  PHASE 1: Intent Translation & Contract Deployment
======================================================================

  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue (upper_bound), ambient_noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: FatigueIndex <= 0.4, Noise <= 80.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority_order array currently contains ['Throughput','Throughput','Throughput'] — three identical, unrecognised entries that do not match any KPI name in the contract (resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, ambient_noise_level). What is the intended lexicographic priority among these three objectives when they conflict? For example: resource_efficiency > operator_fatigue > ambient_noise_level?
       A: Apply lexicographic priority: hard constraints (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB) first, then maximise resource_efficiency. operator_fatigue and ambient_noise_level are treated purely as constraints, not as secondary optimisation targets.
    2. resource_efficiency / resource_input_rate
       Q: Maximising value-per-resource-unit gives the optimiser an incentive to increase absolute resource draw (e.g. run all machines at full speed, raise resource_input_rate to maximum) if doing so raises the ratio. Should total energy or total resource consumption be capped at, say, no more than 110 % of last shift's baseline to prevent unbounded resource escalation?
       A: Cap total resource consumption at 110 % of the previous shift's recorded baseline; if no baseline is available, hold each resource type at its nameplate rated capacity.
    3. value_metric (throughput_or_output_quality_weighted_units)
       Q: The value metric is defined as 'throughput_or_output_quality_weighted_units' but no weighting is given. A high throughput weight rewards speed at the cost of defects; a high quality weight does the opposite. What fraction of delivered value should come from quality inspection pass-rate versus raw unit count — for example, a 60/40 throughput-to-quality split?
       A: Use equal weighting (50 % throughput volume, 50 % quality-pass-rate) until the manager specifies otherwise; scrap and rework units are counted as zero delivered value per the existing assumption.
    4. operator_fatigue (KPI direction vs hard constraint dual encoding)
       Q: Operator fatigue appears as both a KPI with direction 'upper_bound at 0.4' and an identical hard constraint ≤ 0.4. The manager's intent was to keep fatigue 'low', which implies active minimisation below 0.4 — not merely avoiding breach. Should the optimiser minimise fatigue as a soft secondary objective (e.g. target ≤ 0.3), or only enforce the hard ceiling of 0.4?
