    A 25% demand surge has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric constraints simultaneously: the Fatigue Index reached 0.5424 (limit 0.40, excess +0.1424 / +35.6% over cap) and the Noise level reached 82.2 dB (limit 80.0 dB, excess +2.2 dB / +2.75% over cap). Because both violations are driven by increased workload intensity and shift density, the system cannot self-resolve the conflict within current resource and scheduling boundaries. Human authorization is required before any remediation path is enacted. Options are ranked from most protective of worker welfare to least.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended) — Workforce Augmentation via Supplementary Shift: Onboard temporary or contract workers to absorb the 25% demand increase. Redistribute production across an additional partial shift so that per-worker task load and noise-exposure duration fall back within compliance thresholds. Fatigue Index returns to ~0.38 (below 0.40) and average noise exposure drops to ~78 dB once load is split. Requires HR and procurement lead-time of approximately 3–5 business days, introducing a minor schedule gap.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Demand Phasing with Acoustic Engineering Controls: Split the 25% demand increment across two production cycles: fulfill ~12–13% in the current period and carry the remainder into the next scheduling window. Simultaneously deploy acoustic dampening panels and enforce mandatory PPE (ear protection) protocols to bring effective noise exposure to ≤79.5 dB within the current period. Fatigue Index recovers to ~0.39 under the reduced throughput target. This fully preserves both human-centric limits but defers a portion of demand, impacting on-time delivery by roughly 10%.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort) — Time-Bounded Supervised Constraint Relaxation: Temporarily raise the Fatigue Index ceiling to 0.55 and the Noise ceiling to 83.0 dB for a strictly defined window (maximum 10 working days). Requires mandatory compensatory measures: additional 15-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes, daily audiometric spot-checks, a named safety officer on each shift, and explicit written sign-off from the production manager and occupational health representative. A review gate at day 5 must confirm no adverse health indicators before continuation is authorized. This option meets full demand on schedule but does NOT preserve human-centric constraints — it constitutes a formal, auditable deviation.
      Preserves human constraints: No

  [Manager] Decision: Option C
    Rationale: Prioritizing human well-being over deadline
