  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Distribute Demand Across an Additional Authorized Shift: Authorize a new shift window (e.g., extended evening or Saturday shift) to absorb the 20% demand increment. By spreading workload across more clock hours rather than intensifying existing shifts, per-shift FatigueIndex is projected to return to ≤0.40 and noise exposure to ≤80.0 dB with no changes to safety limits. A partial delivery delay of approximately 10% against the current deadline is expected. This is the lowest-risk path: no constraint relaxation, no new engineering investment, and full reversibility.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2: Engineering Controls — Acoustic Attenuation and Mandatory Job Rotation: Implement acoustic shielding on high-noise equipment and introduce a structured job-rotation protocol (workers cycle off high-intensity stations at defined intervals) to bring both Noise and FatigueIndex within limits at the elevated demand rate. An estimated 8% reduction in net output rate is required to accommodate rotation rest periods. No safety limits are modified; all remediation is process-level. Implementation lead time of 1–3 days must be factored into the revised schedule. Suitable if an additional shift is not feasible.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Temporary Constraint Relaxation with Documented Compensatory Safeguards: Request formal governance sign-off to temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.50 and the Noise ceiling to 83.0 dB for a strictly bounded production window tied to the demand surge. Compensatory controls — mandatory extended rest breaks between high-intensity periods, mandatory hearing PPE, and daily occupational-health check-ins — must be activated and logged before the schedule resumes. This option closes the deadline gap entirely but constitutes a deliberate relaxation of human-centric constraints and requires documented manager authorization, a defined expiry date, and a post-window review. It must not become a recurring baseline.
