  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority list contains 'Throughput' three times, but no KPI in this contract is named 'Throughput' — the KPIs are resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue_index, and ambient_noise_level. Which of those three KPIs should rank first, second, and third when the optimiser must make a trade-off (e.g., gaining 5% efficiency at the cost of raising fatigue from 0.35 to 0.39)?
       A: Priority order set to [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue_index, ambient_noise_level], reflecting the manager's stated emphasis on delivered value first, then operator wellbeing, then noise compliance.
    2. resource_efficiency / threshold
       Q: resource_efficiency is set to maximise with no upper bound, meaning the optimiser may push machine cycle rates or labour intensity to extremes until the fatigue or noise hard constraints are hit. Should a maximum pace multiplier or machine utilisation cap be set (e.g., no more than 95% rated speed) to leave a safety margin before the 0.4 fatigue ceiling or 80 dB noise limit is breached?
       A: Machine utilisation capped at 90% of rated capacity and labour pace at 1.0× baseline, preventing the optimiser from saturating resource use right up to the constraint boundary.
    3. value_definition (monetary vs. production units)
       Q: 'Delivered value' is defined as 'monetary or production units', but these lead to different decisions: monetary optimisation favours high-margin, low-volume SKUs while production-unit optimisation favours throughput volume. Which unit should be used when the current shift's variant mix includes products with different margins?
       A: Delivered value measured in production output units (parts or batches completed), making the metric variant-agnostic and compatible with real-time shop-floor data.
    4. operator_fatigue_index direction vs. constraint floor
       Q: operator_fatigue_index is both a hard constraint (≤ 0.4) and a KPI to minimise, but minimising fatigue toward zero may force the optimiser to severely underload operators, sacrificing resource efficiency. Should fatigue optimisation stop once a lower comfort floor is reached (e.g., cease trading efficiency for further fatigue reductions below 0.20)?
       A: Fatigue minimisation treated as a soft target with a floor of 0.20; the optimiser is free to allow fatigue between 0.20–0.40 if it improves resource efficiency, provided the hard constraint of ≤ 0.40 is never violated.
    5. context / shift_duration
       Q: The temporal scope is 'single_shift' but no shift length is specified. Cumulative fatigue and the time-weighted average noise calculation (dBA over shift) both scale with shift duration — an 8-hour shift and a 12-hour shift can make the same activity pattern feasible in one case and infeasible in the other. What is the planned shift length in hours?
       A: Shift duration assumed to be 8 hours (standard single shift), used as the normalisation window for both the fatigue accumulation model and the time-weighted noise average.

