  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Distribute load across additional shifts and stagger high-noise tasks: Add one supplementary shift to absorb the 25% demand increase, distributing worker hours so no individual shift exceeds fatigue thresholds. Stagger high-noise operations (e.g., machining, pressing) to non-overlapping time windows to keep instantaneous noise exposure within 80 dB limits. No constraint limits are relaxed. Estimated schedule extension of 1–2 production cycles may create a modest deadline gap, but full human-centric compliance is maintained. Requires authorization for overtime staffing budget.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 (Moderate): Deploy engineering noise controls and mandatory micro-rest rotations: Install temporary acoustic barriers and enforce mandatory 10-minute rest rotations every 50 minutes to reduce effective FatigueIndex accumulation. With validated engineering controls, the operational Noise ceiling may be administratively raised to 83.0 dB per OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95 engineering-control equivalency provisions, provided workers use rated PPE (minimum NRR-25 earmuffs). FatigueIndex limit is softened to 0.50 to accommodate the demand surge while remaining below clinical risk thresholds. Partial demand fulfillment is achievable within the original delivery window.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 (Last Resort): Temporary constraint relaxation with enhanced real-time monitoring: Temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.62 and the Noise ceiling to 85.0 dB for a strictly bounded window (not to exceed 72 hours) to fulfill the full demand surge. This option requires mandatory real-time biometric fatigue monitoring, continuous decibel logging with automated shutdown triggers, a post-shift medical check-in protocol, and explicit written sign-off from the production manager and EHS officer. Full demand can be met with no deadline gap, but this option formally relaxes human-centric constraint guarantees and must be logged as a governance exception in the CBPA audit trail.
