  [Escalation] Conflict summary:
    A 20.0% surge in production demand has generated a schedule that cannot satisfy current human-centric constraints. Two violations are present: (1) FatigueIndex reached 0.5912 against a hard ceiling of 0.40, an overage of 0.1912 units (47.8% above limit), indicating workers would be exposed to unsustainable physical strain; (2) Noise exposure reached 83.8 dB against a ceiling of 80.0 dB, an overage of 3.8 dB (4.75% above limit), breaching occupational health thresholds. The current schedule is rejected. Human-centric constraints cannot be auto-relaxed without explicit production manager authorization. Three ranked remediation options follow, ordered by degree of constraint preservation.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Split Shift with Worker Rotation (Recommended): Introduce a second production shift and implement mandatory worker rotation across noise-intensive and physically demanding stations. Spreading the 20% demand surge across additional shift hours reduces per-worker fatigue accumulation and limits continuous noise exposure duration. Requires hiring or redeploying 15–20% additional shift labor. Projected FatigueIndex drops to approximately 0.37 and Noise exposure time-weighted average falls to approximately 78.5 dB, both within limits. Lead time to implement: 3–5 business days for scheduling and onboarding.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Partial Demand Deferral with Priority Triage: Fulfill the highest-priority 82–85% of demand within the current scheduling window while deferring the remaining 15–18% to a subsequent window. This keeps the production rate within a range where FatigueIndex stays at or below 0.40 and Noise remains at or below 80.0 dB. The production manager should coordinate with sales and logistics to identify which orders can be deferred with least contractual penalty. No constraint relaxation is required. This is the fastest-to-implement option with zero worker health risk, but incurs a planned partial delivery shortfall.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Temporary Supervised Constraint Relaxation (Requires Explicit Approval): As a last-resort option subject to production manager sign-off and regulatory review, temporarily relax FatigueIndex to 0.55 (from 0.40) and Noise ceiling to 82.0 dB (from 80.0 dB) for a bounded period not to exceed 5 consecutive working days. Mandatory mitigations must accompany this option: mandatory PPE upgrade to noise-attenuating equipment (NRR >= 25 dB), enforced 15-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes, daily fatigue self-assessment check-ins, and real-time biometric monitoring where available. This option meets 100% of demand on schedule but exposes workers to elevated health risk and may trigger occupational health compliance review. This option MUST NOT be activated without documented manager authorization and must be logged in the governance audit trail.
      Preserves human constraints: No

