    4. operator_fatigue (KPI minimize vs. hard constraint at identical limit 0.4)
       Q: Operator fatigue is listed as both a minimize KPI and a hard constraint, both at the same threshold of 0.4: should the optimiser actively drive fatigue as low as possible (e.g. target ≤ 0.25) even at the cost of resource efficiency, or should 0.4 simply serve as a ceiling while efficiency is maximised within that region?
       A: Treat 0.4 as the inviolable hard ceiling only; the minimize KPI is used solely as a tiebreaker when resource_efficiency values are within 1% of each other, avoiding any sacrifice of efficiency for fatigue reduction that is already within bounds.
    5. assumptions.resource_scope (direct labor as a resource)
       Q: Direct labour is included in the resource denominator of efficiency: if reducing operator hours or headcount improves the ratio, the optimiser may be incentivised to cut labour, which concentrates workload on remaining operators and pushes fatigue toward the 0.4 limit — should labour hours be held fixed for this shift, or is intra-shift reallocation across workstations permitted?
       A: Fix total scheduled operator headcount and contracted hours for the shift; allow workstation reassignment between operators but treat aggregate labour-hours as a constant, so the fatigue constraint cannot be undermined by labour-reduction moves that the optimiser discovers as efficiency levers.

