       A: Resources are converted to monetary cost using shift-level standard cost rates (energy: £0.28/kWh, labor: £20/hour, material: £1.00/kg); delivered value is measured in £-equivalent output; the ratio becomes £-output / £-resource. Rates are updated from the shift baseline before each run.
    3. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / unbounded maximisation
       Q: Maximising value-per-resource with no throughput ceiling or resource floor could push the schedule to run equipment at maximum rated speed or reduce staffing to a single operator — is there a maximum acceptable cycle-rate multiplier relative to the shift baseline (e.g., no more than 1.20×) or a minimum resource consumption floor (e.g., energy ≥ 60 % of baseline) to prevent physically unsafe or quality-degrading operating points?
       A: Cycle rate is capped at 1.15× the shift baseline and total resource consumption is bounded below at 70 % of the baseline shift budget, preventing both runaway throughput escalation and resource-starvation artefacts.
    4. operator_fatigue_index / rest-break schedule
       Q: A fatigue ceiling of 0.4 (on a 0–1 scale) over a full shift implies a specific recovery cadence — does the 0.4 limit apply as a time-averaged value across the shift, or as a never-exceeded instantaneous ceiling, and are mandatory rest breaks (e.g., 10 min per 90 min of work) already encoded in the baseline or must the optimiser schedule them?
       A: The 0.4 limit is treated as a never-exceeded instantaneous ceiling. The optimiser inserts mandatory 10-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes of continuous work; fatigue index is modelled as accumulating linearly and recovering at 50 % of the accumulation rate during breaks. If this schedule is infeasible within the shift window, the optimiser flags the contract as infeasible rather than relaxing the constraint.
    5. ambient_noise_level / measurement scope
       Q: The 80 dB(A) limit is defined as a shop-floor average — if one workstation reaches 87 dB while the floor average stays at 79 dB, is that compliant, or must every individual operator position remain below 80 dB throughout the shift?
       A: The 80 dB(A) limit is applied as a per-workstation constraint, not a floor average, to protect against localised overexposure; if per-station sensors are unavailable, the tightest measured zone is used as a conservative proxy for all stations in that cell.

