======================================================================
  PHASE 1: Intent Translation & Contract Deployment
======================================================================

  Manager says: "Increase delivered value per resource consumed for this shift, while keeping operator fatigue low and noise under 80dB."
  Working mode: LEGISLATOR

  [L1] Contract C1 elicited:
    KPIs:        resource_efficiency (maximize), operator_fatigue (upper_bound), noise_level (upper_bound)
    Constraints: FatigueIndex <= 0.4, Noise <= 80.0, shift_duration <= 480.0
    Priorities:  Throughput > Throughput > Throughput

  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority list contains ["Throughput","Throughput","Throughput"] but no KPI named 'Throughput' is defined — should the priority order be ["resource_efficiency","operator_fatigue","noise_level"], or is throughput (units/hour) an additional objective that should be added to kpi_targets?
       A: Priority order is re-mapped to the three defined KPIs in the order they appear in kpi_targets: resource_efficiency > operator_fatigue > noise_level. No standalone throughput objective is added unless the manager confirms it.
    2. resource_efficiency / resource_types (energy cap)
       Q: 'Maximize resource_efficiency' has no upper bound on any individual resource type — if the optimiser raises machine_speed to lift output, energy draw can increase ~30–40 % above baseline; is there a per-shift energy budget (kWh) or a maximum machine_speed multiplier you want enforced?
       A: A soft penalty is applied when any single resource type exceeds 120 % of the prior-shift rolling average, converting it to a secondary hard constraint until the manager supplies an explicit limit.
    3. operator_fatigue.threshold (measurement window)
       Q: Is the 0.4 fatigue ceiling a continuous never-exceed limit (the fatigue index must stay ≤ 0.4 at every instant during the shift), or a time-weighted average over the full 480-minute shift — because a continuous cap drastically restricts rotation-schedule flexibility versus an end-of-shift average?
       A: The 0.4 limit is treated as a continuous hard cap (no instantaneous breach permitted), which is the more conservative interpretation consistent with the stated constraint_precedence assumption.
    4. noise_level.threshold (averaging method)
       Q: Is the 80 dB limit an A-weighted time-weighted average (TWA) over the shift, or a never-exceed instantaneous ceiling — a TWA allows brief exceedances offset by quieter periods, potentially permitting equipment selections that spike to 85–90 dB for short intervals?
       A: The 80 dB limit is interpreted as a continuous TWA measured over any rolling 15-minute window, prohibiting any 15-minute block from averaging above 80 dB(A), balancing practicality with worker-safety intent.
    5. value_proxy (quality_yield_rate weighting / quality floor)
       Q: The value proxy 'units_produced × quality_yield_rate' gives no minimum acceptable yield rate — the optimiser could accept, say, 70 % yield at high speed if it still maximises the ratio; is there a minimum quality_yield_rate (e.g. ≥ 95 %) below which output should not count as delivered value?
       A: A hard floor of quality_yield_rate ≥ 0.90 is enforced; any production cycle yielding below 90 % is excluded from the value numerator and flagged for supervisor review.

