    3. resource_efficiency vs. operator_fatigue_index (directional conflict)
       Q: Maximising value-per-resource typically requires higher operator pace or longer active work periods, which pushes the fatigue index upward toward the 0.4 ceiling; when the optimiser is within ~10% of the fatigue limit, should it throttle cycle pace (accepting an efficiency loss of roughly 5–15%) or should it schedule a mandatory micro-break (losing ~3–5 min of productive time) to keep fatigue headroom?
       A: When the projected fatigue index exceeds 0.35 (87.5% of the 0.4 limit), the optimiser inserts a 5-minute recovery micro-break and reduces cycle rate to 95% of the current rate; throughput loss is accepted over any fatigue-constraint violation.
    4. assumptions.resource_definition (normalization basis)
       Q: The resource aggregate can be normalized on either a cost basis (favouring reduction of expensive peak-tariff energy) or a weight basis (favouring reduction of heavy raw materials); which normalization should the optimiser use, and are all three components — labour-hours, energy (kWh), and raw material — weighted equally or by a given ratio (e.g., 3:2:5)?
       A: A cost basis is used with equal weight assigned to each resource component normalised to its standard unit cost as recorded in the site's ERP system; if ERP data are unavailable, equal proportional weights are applied.
    5. context.shift_definition (exact duration)
       Q: The contract inherits shift duration from the site schedule and assumes 'typically 8 hours', but many sites run 10- or 12-hour shifts; the fatigue index of 0.4 calibrated for 8 hours may be systematically breached on longer shifts — what is the actual shift length for this optimisation run, and should the fatigue threshold be prorated (e.g., 0.33 for a 10-hour shift)?
       A: Shift duration is fixed at 8 hours (480 minutes); if site schedule data indicate a different duration, the fatigue threshold is linearly prorated as 0.4 × (8 / actual_shift_hours) and the optimiser uses the prorated value as its hard constraint.

