       A: Priority order is corrected to ['resource_efficiency', 'operator_fatigue', 'noise_level'] in that sequence; no standalone throughput KPI is added unless the manager confirms otherwise.
    2. resource_efficiency.threshold
       Q: Resource efficiency is set to maximise with no upper bound or target ceiling — should a maximum improvement cap be specified (e.g. no more than 20–25% above the prior-shift baseline) to prevent the optimiser from consuming all available constraint slack in fatigue and noise simultaneously just to squeeze marginal efficiency gains?
       A: Efficiency is maximised subject only to the existing hard constraints (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB) with no additional efficiency ceiling applied; the optimiser may drive both constraints to their limits.
    3. context.horizon / shift_duration
       Q: The contract scope is 'single_shift' but no concrete shift duration is defined — is this an 8-hour standard shift, a 10-hour extended shift, or a 12-hour shift? The answer changes the permissible fatigue accumulation rate per hour and the cumulative noise dose ceiling (e.g. 80 dB for 8 h vs. a lower equivalent limit for 10–12 h under OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95).
       A: Shift duration is assumed to be 8 hours; fatigue and noise thresholds are interpreted relative to an 8-hour exposure window with no overtime extension.
    4. operator_fatigue (conflict with efficiency maximisation)
       Q: Maximising resource efficiency typically requires higher cycle rates or eliminated idle time, which accelerates operator fatigue toward the 0.4 hard limit. If fatigue reaches 0.4 before the shift ends, should the optimiser hold the current pace (capping further efficiency gains), or may it insert job-rotation or mandatory micro-breaks to reset fatigue and resume higher-efficiency operation?
       A: When fatigue hits 0.4 the work rate is throttled to hold fatigue at the boundary for the remainder of the shift; no job-rotation or break-insertion logic is activated unless separately configured.
    5. noise_level.threshold (measurement scope)
       Q: Is the 80 dB limit measured at the operator's ear (personal occupational exposure), as a continuous ambient average across the production floor, or at the machine boundary? The feasible operating envelope can differ by 4–8 dB depending on measurement point, which could make the constraint either easily satisfied or binding at normal production rates.
       A: Noise is evaluated as a continuous A-weighted ambient average at the nearest occupied operator workstation, consistent with the 'continuous_ambient_A_weighted_dB_averaged_over_shift' assumption already declared in the contract.

