  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Workforce Augmentation and Load Redistribution (Recommended): Expand the active workforce by engaging qualified temporary or cross-trained workers to absorb the additional 25% demand load. Redistribute task assignments so that no individual worker's accumulated fatigue exceeds the 0.40 index threshold. Simultaneously, stagger high-noise operations across shifts and introduce brief mandatory quiet periods to hold floor-average noise below 80.0 dB. Engineering controls (acoustic dampening panels, equipment maintenance cycle acceleration) should be deployed in parallel. This option fully preserves all human-centric constraints and is expected to introduce only a minor scheduling lag of approximately 6–9% of the horizon while the augmented workforce is onboarded and rostered.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Partial Fulfillment with Expedited External Sourcing: Fulfill the maximum volume achievable (estimated ~78–82% of total demand) using the existing workforce operating strictly within FatigueIndex ≤ 0.40 and Noise ≤ 80.0 dB limits. The remaining 18–22% of demand is routed to a qualified external supplier or contract manufacturer on an expedited basis. This approach guarantees zero constraint violations, protects worker wellbeing, and avoids a full production halt. The trade-off is a cost premium on outsourced units and a potential delivery gap of roughly 12–18% of the order if the external supplier's lead time exceeds internal capacity recovery. Recommended when augmenting the workforce is not feasible within the required timeframe.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Time-Limited Constraint Relaxation with Enhanced Compensatory Controls (Last Resort): As a strictly time-bounded exception (not to exceed 5 consecutive working days), temporarily raise the FatigueIndex ceiling to 0.55 and the Noise ceiling to 83.5 dB. Mandatory compensatory controls must be activated immediately: (a) ISO-rated hearing protection (≥ NRR 25) issued and enforced for all floor personnel, (b) daily fatigue assessments with automatic pull-out authority for any worker scoring above 0.52, (c) 20% extended rest intervals between high-intensity task blocks, and (d) an independent safety officer sign-off required each shift. This option meets 100% of demand with no delivery gap but constitutes a deliberate, documented relaxation of human-centric constraints and carries elevated regulatory and liability exposure. It must be logged as a formal governance override, reviewed by EHS, and closed out with a corrective action report upon expiry.
