  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order
       Q: The priority_order field lists 'Throughput' three times instead of ranking the three actual KPIs — should the intended priority be (1) resource_efficiency, (2) operator_fatigue, (3) noise_level, or a different ordering among these?
       A: Priority order is reset to [resource_efficiency, operator_fatigue, noise_level] to match the stated primary and secondary objectives; raw throughput is not treated as a standalone objective.
    2. resource_efficiency / delivered_value_metric
       Q: Should 'delivered value' be measured as production volume (units output) or as value-added margin (e.g. £ per resource-unit), and — if the shift runs mixed product variants — should each variant be weighted by its margin when computing the efficiency numerator?
       A: Delivered value is treated as total production volume (units) aggregated over the shift with equal weight per unit; variant margin weighting is not applied unless a price or margin table is explicitly provided.
    3. resource_efficiency / resource_scope aggregation
       Q: The resource denominator combines energy (kWh), raw material (kg), machine time (h), and labour hours — what normalisation weights should collapse these into a single 'resource-unit' (e.g. site standard cost weights in £, or physical-unit weighting)?
       A: Resources are aggregated using site standard cost weights (£/unit); if no cost table is available, only energy (kWh) and labour hours are included at equal monetary weight and a feasibility warning is raised.
    4. operator_fatigue / measurement granularity
       Q: Is the fatigue hard constraint of 0.4 a shift-end average, a rolling worst-case window (e.g. peak over any 30-minute interval), or a real-time instantaneous ceiling — since an elevated cycle rate could spike peak fatigue above 0.4 even when the shift average remains below it?
       A: Fatigue is enforced as a real-time instantaneous ceiling: the constraint is violated at any moment fatigue exceeds 0.4, not only on a shift-average basis.
    5. resource_efficiency / upper bound on absolute resource consumption
       Q: Maximising the efficiency ratio has no cap on total resource draw — if running at full machine utilisation raises the ratio by ~5% but increases total energy consumption by ~40% above the baseline shift budget, is that acceptable, or should absolute consumption per resource category be capped?
       A: Total consumption for each resource category is capped at 110% of the site baseline shift budget; efficiency-ratio improvements that require breaching this cap are excluded from the feasible solution space.

