    - Option 2 — Partial Demand Deferral to Next Production Window: Accept the current maximum feasible throughput that keeps FatigueIndex ≤ 0.40 and Noise ≤ 80.0 dB, and reschedule approximately 20% of the demand surge into the next available production window. This approach requires negotiation with the customer or internal supply chain stakeholders to accept a split delivery. All worker safety thresholds are maintained in full, and no constraint waivers are required. Suitable when delivery flexibility exists and customer lead-time tolerance allows partial shipment.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Supervised Temporary Constraint Relaxation (Last Resort): With explicit safety officer sign-off and the following mandatory mitigations, temporarily raise FatigueIndex to 0.50 and Noise ceiling to 83.0 dB for a single production run: (a) mandatory 20-minute recovery breaks every 90 minutes, (b) issue and enforce Class-B hearing protection (PPE) for all floor personnel, (c) real-time physiological monitoring with automatic line-stop triggers, and (d) post-shift wellness check-ins. This option meets the full demand deadline but constitutes a formal constraint waiver and must be logged in the CBPA governance ledger. It does NOT preserve human-centric constraints at their approved levels and carries residual health risk. Use only if Options 1 and 2 are operationally infeasible.
